      ---------------------------------------------------------------
            Throughput regression checking for the wrapper API
      ---------------------------------------------------------------


1. Purpose
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The speed sweep (test/speed.sh) measures the wrapper's throughput but keeps no
memory: a run only helps if somebody remembers what the numbers used to be.
The speed-check.sh script supplies that memory in the form of a machine-local
baseline and turns the comparison into an exit code, so a performance regression
can fail a validation run the same way a compiler warning fails test-rebase.sh.

Two checks run on every invocation.  The per-group check compares the rate of
every measured combination against the recorded baseline and it tolerates a
configurable drop, which absorbs the run-to-run noise of a developer machine.
The scaling check divides the rate of the last thread group by the rate of the
first within every instance count and requires a minimum ratio; this guards
the lock-scaling property that is documented in README-meter_performance without
depending on any absolute numbers, so that it holds on any hardware even when
the baseline is stale.


2. Usage
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  % ./speed-check.sh [-b baseline] [-c test-cfg] [-g groups] [-h]
                     [-i instances] [-R] [-r runtime_ms] [-s min_scale]
                     [-T tolerance_pct] [-t test-program]

The first run, or any run with -R, records the baseline; later runs compare
against it.  The default settings measure the 1, 2 and 4 instance counts in
combination with the 1, 4, 32 and 256 thread groups for ten seconds each, with
a fifteen percent tolerance and a minimum scaling ratio of 1.2.  The measured
program is test/otel-c-wrapper-test when it exists, the debug variant otherwise,
or the program named with -t, treated as the debug variant when its name ends
in _dbg, and it loads test/otel-cfg.yml unless -c names another configuration;
the baseline records the producing variant, the CPU model of the machine and the
wrapper version-build; a comparison across variants is refused while a differing
CPU model only produces a warning, because the results are then probably not
comparable.

The baseline lives in test/_speed_baseline by default, which the underscore
pattern of scripts/distclean removes: after a distclean the next run records a
fresh baseline instead of comparing.  A re-record with -R keeps the previous
baseline under a dated -old suffix instead of overwriting it, and a comparison
run writes the rates it measured to a dated file next to the baseline, in the
baseline format, so an interesting run can later serve as a baseline via -b.
Exit codes follow sysexits.h: 70 for a detected regression, 78 for a baseline
that does not cover the request, 73 for a baseline rotation that failed, 69
for a missing test program, and 64 for a usage error.


3. Interpretation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A REGRESSION verdict on a thread group means that the throughput dropped beyond
the tolerance against the recorded past of this machine; a REGRESSION on the
scaling line means worker parallelism collapsed, which points at a newly added
serialization point rather than at general slowdown.  Re-record the baseline
with -R after an intended performance change, after a hardware or toolchain
change, and treat any single-group failures on a loaded machine with suspicion
before believing them.
